From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Biggart <swbiggart@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:39:33 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 25/46] flashnext cuda: a rank waits for its peer's load before
 the first collective

At --tp 2 each rank ran the benchmark gather, its first collective, as soon as
its own weights were in, under the collective's 30 s bound. Two ranks whose
loads finished more than that apart could never start: the faster one stopped
with RankTimeout and the start failed.

Session.afterLoad first runs a load-ready exchange (room: the same frame, its
validation and its 10-minute bound; a closed link ends it at once), then the
gather. The exchange at the end of the load still runs, so the ranks now
exchange ready twice. The gather stays where it was, so NCCL's buffers, the
scratch and the memory plan come in the same order.

A host test over a socket pair shows the first collective waits for a peer
past the collective's bound and that a peer leaving ends the wait before any
collective; flashnext-rank gains late and gone modes on two GPUs.
